hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.23.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Nothing played from a turntable, a web radio or an AirPlay stream was ever
    scrobbled. Last.fm's rule is half the track or four minutes, whichever
    comes first, and the four-minute half of it needs no track length -- but
    the check that applied it required one before it ran at all, and those
    sources do not have one. On a device with an analog input the metadata log
    showed the same line all day, a length of "N/A" and Now Playing updated
    for each track, with not one scrobble attempt behind it. Sources that do
    report a length -- Spotify, Roon, local files -- were unaffected, which is
    why this went unnoticed.

  * A track from such a source is now also scrobbled when it ends, which for
    an unknown length is the only moment that can judge it: a three-minute
    side never reaches four minutes while it plays. The floor is Last.fm's own
    thirty seconds, so lifting the needle early still does not count as a
    listen, and a track already scrobbled while playing is not sent twice.

  * A stop now ends the track for players that never clear the song they
    announced. Bluetooth is one, and its AVRCP metadata frequently carries no
    duration, so an A2DP track that was stopped rather than followed by
    another was judged by neither rule.

  * A reported length of zero is read as unknown. RAAT and the generic player
    report it that way for a stream whose length they do not know, and taken
    literally it says every moment is past half the track -- so the track
    scrobbled having been played for no time at all.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:20:00 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.23.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Marking a song as a favourite could write it into the wrong database. When
    the settings database could not be opened at the path it was configured for
    -- a corrupt file, a directory that cannot be created -- the store kept the
    connection it had opened before, and POST /api/favourites/add answered 200
    while the favourite landed in another database entirely. On a device this
    merged the two halves' state: a favourite added through the metadata side
    was found afterwards in the player daemon's own settings.db. A store that
    cannot be configured is now unusable, so the call fails visibly instead of
    succeeding against a file nobody asked for. The same fault, and the same
    fix, applied to the attribute cache.

  * The two errors about /var/lib/audiocontrol/cache/attributes.db logged at
    every start are gone. Neither the settings database nor the attribute cache
    now opens -- or creates -- anything before it has been told where its files
    belong, so the first access no longer touches a hard-coded path and no
    longer reports a permissions failure on a directory the daemon was never
    configured to use.

