hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.23.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Installing the package now reloads nginx, which is what 0.23.0 was
    missing. The metadata prefixes moved to the second daemon's port in that
    release, but nothing told nginx to read the new routing, so it went on
    proxying with the configuration it had loaded when it last started - on a
    device that had been up a while, a configuration weeks older than the
    upgrade itself. The result was an installation that looked clean and was
    not: /api/audiocontrol/favourites/providers,
    /api/audiocontrol/coverart/methods and /api/audiocontrol/lastfm/status
    answered 404 through nginx, and the web interface's system information page
    reported favourites and cover art as errored providers, with nothing in any
    log to explain it. Reloading nginx by hand fixed it completely; the package
    now does it as part of the install. Any package dropping a snippet into
    /etc/nginx/hifiberry-api.d is picked up the same way, once per install run
    rather than once per package, and the configuration is checked before the
    reload so a mistake elsewhere in it is reported rather than applied.

  * A failed genre lookup is no longer recorded as a fact about the album.
    Album genres from MusicBrainz are cached, empty answers included, so an
    album the service holds no genres for is not asked about on every library
    load. But the lookup reported a failure and a genuinely empty answer as the
    same empty list, and the empty list was written to the attribute cache with
    no expiry - so a sweep that ran while MusicBrainz was refusing requests
    marked the albums it touched as permanently genre-less, and never asked
    again on any later sweep or restart. In one sweep against an unhealthy
    provider three quarters of the albums touched ended up recorded that way.
    A lookup now says whether it got an answer: an answer is cached as before,
    a failure - a request error, an unreadable response, or lookups being
    disabled - is not cached at all, so the next sweep asks again, and the
    sweep's completion log says how many lookups went unanswered instead of
    reporting a quiet zero.

    Caches written by earlier versions may still hold these entries. Removing
    the empty album::genres:: rows lets those albums be looked up again;
    entries that hold genres are correct and worth keeping.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 08 Sep 2026 22:15:00 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.23.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Enrichment progress is now reported by the daemon that does the work.
    GET /api/metadata/background/jobs lists the artist and album metadata
    jobs; /api/audiocontrol/background/jobs goes on listing the player
    daemon's own library and thumbnail jobs. The two registries are separate
    and neither answers for the other, so a client that polled the second for
    enrichment progress finds it there no longer.

  * Rate limiting now bounds how many requests are open at a provider, not
    just how often a new one starts. The limiter waited out the minimum
    spacing and then let go, so the request itself was unaccounted for: when a
    provider answered slowly the next request went out on schedule anyway and
    the outstanding ones piled up until the provider started refusing them.
    Measured over an enrichment sweep of an 11,858-album library, 110 of 114
    MusicBrainz responses were HTTP 503 - 96% of the lookups in that sweep
    returned nothing, while the same queries made one at a time still answered
    normally. A request now holds a permit for as long as it is in flight, so
    the configured rate is the rate the provider actually sees.

    The wait also no longer blocks other providers. It used to sleep holding
    one global lock, so a slow MusicBrainz held up Last.fm scrobbling too;
    each provider now waits on its own.

    Separately, fanart.tv had a rate limit registered and logged but applied
    to nothing - every fanart.tv request went out unspaced and unbounded while
    the log reported a limit it was not using. It is applied now.

    The visible effect of all three is that enrichment of a large library gets
    further per sweep. A lookup that comes back 503 contributes nothing and is
    not retried until the next pass, so the genres, images and artist detail
    it would have filled in simply stayed missing.

  * audiocontrol_dump_cache now defaults to the cache it can read. Its
    shortcut flags select prefixes only the metadata daemon writes, while its
    default directory was the player daemon's, so an invocation without
    --cache-dir reported an empty cache rather than saying it was looking in
    the wrong place.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 08 Sep 2026 20:30:00 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.23.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The metadata half now runs as its own process. External lookups, cover
    art, favourites and Last.fm scrobbling move out of the player daemon into
    audiocontrol-metadata, which listens on loopback port 1084 and reaches the
    player daemon over HTTP; nginx forwards the metadata prefixes there, so
    client URLs are unchanged. Both daemons ship in this one package and are
    enabled, upgraded and rolled back together, so no device runs one half
    against another version of the other.

    What a user sees of this is nothing, beyond a second unit in systemctl.
    No URL changes, no account has to be linked again, and nothing needs to be
    configured for the two to find each other. One setting does move, and only
    for an installation that set its own provider API keys; that is the entry
    below. Otherwise this is the point of the release: the daemons were
    separated so that a metadata lookup can no longer stall or crash playback,
    not so that anyone would have to manage two of them. If audiocontrol-metadata is stopped, playback, volume,
    the library and the web interface all keep working; what stops is
    enrichment, artist images, favourites and scrobbling.

  * The player daemon is now built without its metadata code rather than
    merely not using it. Left in, it would have brought up a second metadata
    instance of its own on port 1080 - a second set of caches, a second
    enricher and a second scrobbler on the same events, which would have
    scrobbled every track to Last.fm twice.

  * Metadata state moves with it. /var/lib/audiocontrol/metadata now holds the
    metadata daemon's settings database, its image cache and its own
    credential store. The credential store and the settings database - which
    carry the linked accounts, the favourite songs and the chosen artist
    images - are copied from their previous paths on upgrade, so nothing a
    user set up is lost. The player daemon keeps its own files where they
    were, so a rollback to the previous version finds them intact.

    The lookup cache is deliberately not copied. It is rewritten constantly by
    the running daemon, so a copy taken during an upgrade can be torn, and a
    torn SQLite file does not announce itself: the daemon opens it, logs one
    error and then runs with no cache at all, for that boot and every boot
    after it. Starting from an empty cache costs some repeated MusicBrainz and
    TheAudioDB lookups over the following days and nothing else, so that is
    what it does.

  * Provider API keys are configured in metadata.json now, and are not
    migrated. The metadata daemon reads its own file,
    /etc/audiocontrol/metadata.json, so the sections that configure external
    providers belong there: action_plugins.lastfm, and services.lastfm,
    services.musicbrainz, services.theaudiodb, services.fanarttv and
    services.external_coverart. The same sections still exist in
    audiocontrol.json and the player daemon no longer reads any of them.

    This only matters where a key was set by hand. Both files ship with every
    key empty, and empty means the built-in keys, which is what almost every
    device runs. But an upgrade keeps an edited audiocontrol.json and installs
    a fresh metadata.json, and nothing copies values between the two, so a
    hand-set key has to be copied into metadata.json by hand. Left where it
    is, it is simply not read.

    Last.fm is the case that bites, because it fails without looking like a
    failure. The session key the upgrade carries across was issued against the
    user's own API key, so a daemon that falls back to the built-in one has a
    session it cannot use: scrobbles are refused, the account still shows as
    linked, and nothing in the log says why. Copy api_key and api_secret from
    action_plugins.lastfm in audiocontrol.json into the same entry in
    metadata.json, restart audiocontrol-metadata, and scrobbling resumes.

  * The metadata daemon logs to the journal rather than to a file. It does not
    read logging.json, so RUST_LOG or --debug is how its level is set; the
    per-subsystem levels the player daemon supports are not available for it
    yet.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.22.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The player daemon and its external-metadata code now talk to each other
    over HTTP on loopback rather than through in-process calls. Enrichment
    results, library batches, the title-order and artist-split resolvers and
    the Spotify access token all cross the same routes a separate metadata
    daemon will serve. Both halves still ship in this one package and run in
    one process; the split is internal, and no device runs one half without
    the other.

  * WebSocket connections are now kept alive by a server-side ping every 30
    seconds. Before this, a client that only listened was pruned after an
    hour with the socket still open: the browser WebSocket API exposes no way
    to send a ping, so a page that had subscribed and then waited simply went
    deaf, with no error and nothing in any log. Clients no longer need a
    periodic send of their own, though one remains harmless.

  * A daemon that is shutting down now sends a WebSocket close frame (code
    1001) before closing the socket, instead of letting the connection be
    torn down at the end of its grace period. This cuts a stop or a restart
    with a browser attached from about five seconds to about two. Clients
    whose reconnect logic is guarded on an unclean close should reconnect on
    every close instead -- see doc/websocket.md.

  * New routes for the metadata side, mounted both at their existing paths
    and under /api/metadata/: artist detail, the two resolvers and a
    capabilities report. Existing paths are unchanged.

  * New WebSocket event, library_changed, sent when a player's library is
    loaded or reloaded. A client that does not subscribe to it sees no
    difference; one that displays library contents can use it to refresh
    instead of polling. A reload sends it twice -- once as the rebuild starts
    and once when the finished library is readable -- and it is a signal to
    re-read rather than a value to compare: see doc/websocket.md.

  * The library gains a generation token, distinct from its version, so an
    enrichment batch computed against a library that has since reloaded is
    refused rather than merged into the wrong contents.

  * An album artist whose tag names several artists is now split on separators
    while the library loads, and corrected afterwards by the metadata sweep
    instead of being looked up during the load. **You may see this.** The
    first load that meets a new album artist shows the plain split -- so an
    album may briefly list three artists where there is one ("Emerson, Lake &
    Palmer"), or one where there are two -- and the sweep fixes it, the same
    way genres, images and biographies already arrive late on that screen.
    What it buys is a load that no longer makes a MusicBrainz request per
    album, on a library that can hold 200,000 songs. The correction needs
    MusicBrainz lookups enabled, which the shipped configuration has; an
    install with them off sees no change at all, since the old path already
    did a plain split when they were off.

  * GET /api/library/<player>/albums gains album_artist: the album-artist tag
    as the backend reported it, before it was split into artists. Additive,
    and absent where the library recorded none.

  * The player daemon no longer calls the metadata side at all. Every
    connection between the two is now opened by the metadata side, which is
    what lets playback and every player route keep working when it is absent
    or has crashed. The services.metadata section is gone from
    audiocontrol.json; one left in a local configuration file is ignored.

  * GET /api/library/<player>/image/artist:<name> now answers 302 with a
    Location of /api/coverart/artist/<base64>/image, instead of serving the
    image bytes itself. **Follow redirects.** Every browser and every normal
    HTTP client already does, and the destination is the same path the artist
    lists have always given in thumb_url, so a client that follows one sees
    the same picture. Artist art is fetched from providers and stored by the
    metadata side, and the player daemon no longer asks it for anything. One
    thing improves: ?size= now works on artist art, where it was previously
    accepted and ignored.

  * An artist's biography, its source and its banner URL now reach the library
    with the rest of the enrichment sweep, rather than being fetched per
    request. GET /api/library/<player>/artist/by-name/... serves the same
    fields, and they appear once the sweep has reported on that artist, like
    the genres and thumbnails beside them, rather than immediately after a
    lookup that ran during the request.

  * **A biography served from the library is now capped at 2000 bytes**, so a
    long one is cut mid-sentence where it used to be whole. It is not a
    cosmetic limit: the library keeps every biography it is sent for as long as
    it holds the library, at roughly three times the stored text once
    allocation is counted, and a large collection has eight to fifteen thousand
    distinct artists. Unbounded, that is around ninety megabytes of memory on a
    device that may have one gigabyte and is holding the library too. The full
    text is unaffected and still available from GET /api/artist/<name>.

  * **The order of "Artist - Title" in a radio stream is now decided locally,
    and a wrong guess is corrected later rather than before it is shown.** The
    daemon used to ask MusicBrainz which half was the artist before displaying
    anything, which was right the first time and cost a network round trip on
    every track change. It now applies the station's configured order, or the
    one it has learned, or the "Artist - Title" convention -- and reports what
    MusicBrainz says afterwards, as an observation against that station.

    **You may see this**, and on one kind of station you will see it for a
    while. A station that announces "Title - Artist" displays every track the
    wrong way round until it has been learned, and learning needs twenty
    decided observations at ninety-five per cent agreement -- roughly an hour
    of continuous listening. The track playing when a correction arrives keeps
    the split it was given; only later tracks benefit. Setting the order
    outright for that station, through POST /api/player/<player>/splitter/
    <station>, takes effect immediately and beats anything learned.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.21.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The volume percentage reported for an ALSA hardware control now follows a
    perceptual scale rather than the raw mixer position. A hardware mixer's
    raw range is normally linear in decibels, so reading it directly as
    0-100% spread a ~104 dB span evenly across the slider: on an Amp2's
    "Digital" control the mid-point sat at about -52 dB, inaudible, and every
    usable listening level was crushed into the top third of the travel.
    The percentage now follows the cube-root loudness curve that alsamixer
    and PulseAudio apply, which puts 50% near -17.6 dB and moves the -40 dB
    to -10 dB window from 29% of the travel to 47%. Reported against an Amp2
    driven over AirPlay, where the same hardware state that MPD and the phone
    both called 26% was shown as 66%.

  * The decibel value reported by /api/volume/info is now read from the
    hardware. It was previously interpolated from the percentage against the
    advertised range, so it described the daemon's own mapping rather than
    the control's, and setting a level in dB round-tripped through that same
    interpolation.

  * The advertised decibel range no longer contains an invented figure. A
    control whose bottom step is a hard mute reports ALSA's
    SND_CTL_TLV_DB_GAIN_MUTE sentinel for it; the daemon read that as
    unusable and substituted a fixed -120 dB, which the API then published as
    fact. It now probes for the quietest step carrying a real value, and a
    control with no usable decibel data reports none instead of a guess.

  * Volume changes seen by the polling monitor and those read back from the
    API now go through one conversion, so an event and a subsequent read
    cannot disagree, and a write reports the level the quantised hardware
    settled on rather than the one that was asked for.

  * Minor rather than patch: /api/volume/info gained a volume_scale field,
    and services.volume gained a volume_scale setting ("perceptual", the
    default, or "raw" for the previous behaviour).

    Clients that store a percentage must store the scale with it. The same
    number means a different level in each domain -- 78 is -22.8 dB as a raw
    position and -6.3 dB perceptually, about 16 dB apart -- so a value saved
    by an older version and replayed against this one plays back far louder
    than intended. A missing volume_scale means "raw".

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 10:15:44 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.20.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The daemon now uses jemalloc rather than the system allocator on Linux.
    Measured on a Pi 5 holding a 202,393-song MPD library, same binary and
    same library throughout, only the allocator swapped: glibc settled at
    370 MB resident, jemalloc at 255 MB once loaded and 177 MB half an hour
    later as it returned pages. The glibc process left running for a day had
    reached 1.1 GB. Library load took 720 s either way, so this is not
    trading memory for speed.
  * **Those figures come from LD_PRELOADing an unprefixed jemalloc, which is
    not quite what this change does.** tikv-jemalloc-sys prefixes its symbols
    by default, so `#[global_allocator]` routes Rust's allocations and leaves
    bundled SQLite, OpenSSL, dbus and ALSA on the system allocator. The bulk
    of what was saved looks Rust-side -- the library's own accounting puts its
    data at 75 MB against a 370 MB resident set, i.e. per-allocation overhead
    across roughly a million small strings -- but the shipped configuration
    has not itself been measured on a device. Treat 255 MB as an upper bound
    on the saving, not a promise.
  * Capping glibc's arenas was measured first and does nothing here: 369 MB
    against 370 MB. The memory was never in per-arena free lists, so anyone
    reaching for MALLOC_ARENA_MAX later would be repeating a dead end.
  * The build now pins jemalloc's page size (JEMALLOC_SYS_WITH_LG_PAGE=14,
    in .cargo/config.toml so every build path gets it, not just the
    packaging). Without it jemalloc takes the page size from whatever machine
    built the binary, and a 4 KB-page builder produces one that aborts before
    main() on a 16 KB-page Pi 5 -- "Unsupported system page size", which with
    Restart=on-failure is a restart loop from a package that installed
    perfectly. Reproduced and then fixed on real hardware. 14 is the largest
    page any current target uses; a larger value would be safe but would
    coarsen jemalloc's slabs on the very metric this release improves.
  * Linux only. It is what the devices run; macOS development builds keep the
    system allocator and gain no C build step.

  * The source package no longer carries the .git directory. dpkg-source
    tarballs the whole build directory for a native package and applies its
    default exclusions only when asked, so every source package until now
    shipped the full repository history -- 920 entries, most of the tarball.
    debian/source/options also excludes build and container state, which a
    package built from a development checkout rather than a clean clone would
    otherwise pick up; a local container target directory is routinely several
    gigabytes. .cargo/config.toml is deliberately still included, because the
    packaged build needs the page-size pin as much as any other build path.
 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 05:59:41 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.20.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * An external cover art endpoint can now hand the daemon the image itself,
    instead of a URL for the client to fetch. That was the gap that made the
    previous release's endpoints unusable for anything on a private network:
    the URL reached the phone or browser, which had no route to the host and
    no credential for it. An endpoint may send the image base64-encoded in an
    `images` entry's new `data` field, or set `localize` and let the daemon
    fetch its URL — with the endpoint's configured headers, which the
    metadata fetch never sent. Either way the bytes land in the image cache
    and are served from `/api/imagecache/`, like the artist images already
    were, and are rewritten for `X-Forwarded-Prefix` as an external URL never
    could be.
  * Pass-through remains the default. `localize` is off unless configured, so
    a publicly reachable provider is untouched: copying its images onto an
    appliance's disk to serve them again would cost storage for nothing.
  * The daemon decides an image's type from its bytes rather than from
    anything the endpoint claims, which doubles as a check that the bytes are
    an image at all. A URL that answers with an HTML sign-in page or a JSON
    error is a realistic failure, and storing that would serve a broken
    picture from a URL the daemon vouches for.
  * Failing to obtain artwork the endpoint reported is cached as an error for
    an hour, not as an absence of artwork for a week. The endpoint said there
    was a cover; not getting it is this daemon's problem and must not leave
    the track blank until the negative cache expires. A cached answer whose
    image files have since gone is treated as a miss and looked up again,
    rather than served as a URL that 404s.
  * `max_image_bytes` bounds a single image and at most eight images from one
    answer are considered. Passing URLs through cost a metadata fetch each;
    localising adds a download, a file and a check on every later read, so an
    endpoint answering with hundreds of entries could otherwise turn one song
    change into hours of work.
  * Images stored in the cache are now written through a temporary file and
    renamed. `store_image_with_expiry` created the destination and filled it
    in place, so a client reading an entry while it was being refreshed saw a
    partial file. This affects every caller, not only cover art.
  * A locally served image is now measured for grading instead of being
    treated as a filesystem path and failing to open. An unmeasured image
    scored as neutral rather than on its actual resolution, so every image
    served from `/api/imagecache/` — Shairport cover art included — was
    ranked without regard to its size. Note that measuring can move a score
    either way: a large cover now scores above neutral, and a small
    thumbnail below it, so which provider wins a given track may change.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 16:28:36 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.19.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Cover art can now come from external HTTP endpoints configured at
    runtime, under `services.external_coverart`. Each endpoint is a URL
    template with `{artist}`, `{album}`, `{title}`, `{year}` and `{url}`
    placeholders, its own headers for credentials, and its own timeout and
    cache lifetimes. The response contract is fixed — a JSON body with an
    `images` array — so an endpoint that speaks something else needs an
    adapter in front of it rather than a mapping in this config.
  * An endpoint may declare itself slow, which is what makes a lookup taking
    tens of seconds usable. A slow endpoint is never on the critical path of a
    request: the REST cover art handlers answer it from cache only, and the
    daemon runs the real lookup in the background when the song changes,
    publishing the result as a `song_information_update` event once it
    arrives. A client that wants to pay the wait can ask for it explicitly
    with `?include_slow=true`, which is bounded by the endpoint's configured
    timeout.
  * Cover art providers are now queried in parallel and without holding the
    provider registry lock. The previous fan-out held a global mutex across
    every provider's network I/O, plus a metadata fetch per returned URL, so
    one slow provider stalled every cover art request in the daemon,
    including those for other songs.
  * Answers are cached in three classes with separate lifetimes: an image
    found, a confirmed absence of artwork, and a lookup error. An endpoint
    outage therefore stops mattering within the hour instead of blanking a
    track for the length of the negative cache.
  * `doc/websocket.md` records the `cover_art_source` values a configured
    endpoint produces. The set is open-ended by design; a client should keep
    treating any source other than `station_logo` as real artwork rather
    than matching a fixed list.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 13:37:10 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.18.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * `/api/now-playing` reflects a cover art lookup instead of only the
    WebSocket. The enrichment used to publish the better image as a
    `song_information_update` event and stop there, so the REST response kept
    reporting the station's logo for as long as the stream played, and a
    client that reconnected and re-fetched now-playing regressed to the logo
    even after a WebSocket subscriber had already moved on. The lookup now
    writes into the player's stored song, which both surfaces are built from,
    so a client that only polls sees the same progression a WebSocket
    subscriber sees.
  * A cover art lookup that finishes after its song has moved on is dropped
    rather than applied. The lookup is a network round trip and a radio track
    can end while it is in flight; nothing checked before, so a late answer
    could put the previous track's artwork on the one now playing. The update
    carries the title it was looked up for, and is discarded if that no
    longer matches the song in place.
  * Cover art belonging to a song is never overwritten by a later lookup;
    only a placeholder is, identified by `song.metadata.cover_art_source`.
    This rule previously lived wherever a plugin happened to assign
    cover_art_url, so it applied only where that plugin remembered to check
    it. It now lives once, in the player itself, ahead of every write.
  * What counts as a song change is now one rule for every backend: the title,
    the artist or the stream URL differs from what is already playing. Each
    backend previously carried its own version -- some compared the artist,
    some the album, some neither -- so what a client saw as a new track
    depended on which player it was listening to. Only two backends ever set a
    stream URL, so the artist has to be in the rule: without it a same-titled
    track by a different artist would not register as a change on MPRIS,
    Bluetooth, RAAT or Shairport, and the new track would be reported with the
    previous one's artist, album and cover art for as long as it played.
  * A metadata-only refresh of the song already playing -- cover art arriving
    after the song itself is the ordinary case -- now reaches clients from
    every source that speaks only when something has changed: the generic
    controller, Shairport, librespot, and RAAT's update channel. Several of
    those stored the refresh but the conversion on the way out would have
    dropped it, and some never attempted to propagate it at all. A source that
    instead re-delivers the current state continuously -- MPRIS and Bluetooth
    on a poll, RAAT's metadata pipe on every line it emits -- deliberately does
    not announce a refresh: what looks like one there is the same reading
    again, and announcing it would put an event on the bus per delivery for as
    long as the track played. Whether the source is polled or calls back makes
    no difference to this; whether it speaks only on a change does.
  * Cover art found by a lookup survives on RAAT. Every line down the RAAT
    metadata pipe carries the playback position and a full description of the
    track, so lines arrive throughout a track whether or not anything changed
    -- the player is declared gone after ten seconds without one. Storing each
    line's track unconditionally erased artwork a lookup had found within a
    pipe interval, and published a song change per line for the whole track.
  * A cover art lookup that names its source no longer relabels artwork it did
    not supply. `cover_art_source` was copied out of the lookup's answer even
    when the answer's image had been refused for belonging to the song
    already, which misreported to clients where the displayed artwork came
    from. Provenance now travels with the image or not at all.
  * `song.metadata.cover_art_source` gains the value `"enrichment"`, for
    artwork a lookup supplied without naming itself. The full set of values a
    client can meet is documented in the event contract, together with the
    rule for reading one it does not recognise: `"station_logo"` is the only
    placeholder, everything else is the track's own artwork.
  * Spotify playback no longer jumps back to the start of the track when the
    artist arrives late. The position was reset whenever the song's identity
    changed, and the artist became part of that identity in this release --
    but librespot announces a track first and fills in the artist moments
    later, so an ordinary track was rewound in the middle of playing. The
    reset now follows the track, not its metadata: when both the track playing
    and the one announced carry a Spotify URI, that alone decides, because it
    names the track exactly and does not change as the rest of the fields fill
    in. The URI is optional and can itself arrive late, so where either side
    lacks one the whole of the title, artist and album decides instead --
    which keeps two consecutive same-titled tracks apart rather than running
    the second one on from the first one's position.
  * AirPlay artwork is announced once rather than once per filesystem event.
    The cover art watcher sees a file created and then written, and each of
    those told every client to re-read a song whose artwork had not changed
    between them.
  * Shairport announces a session restart. `SessionStart` cleared the current
    song without telling clients, unlike `SessionEnd`, which did; a client
    that had a track showing when a new AirPlay session began kept showing it
    until the next song arrived. Both events now notify.
  * MPRIS and Bluetooth song changes now reach clients. Both backends rebuild
    their current song from D-Bus inside `get_song()` -- MPRIS also from a
    poll thread -- and neither emitted a change event from there before; MPRIS
    did not notify on a song change at all. An event now fires when the song
    genuinely changes, and only then: a poll that re-reads the track already
    playing stays silent and leaves the stored song alone, so cover art a
    lookup has found is not overwritten a second later by the same reading
    without it.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 16:08:27 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.17.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * TheAudioDB answers the Song cover art method, alongside Last.fm. The two
    cover different ground -- for the radio track that prompted this work
    TheAudioDB knows nothing and Last.fm has a 1200x1200 cover, while for a
    well-known album track both answer -- and the cover art manager grades and
    sorts whatever comes back, so having both is worth more than either.
  * A track TheAudioDB has no picture of falls back to the cover of the album
    its own answer names. It knows many more tracks than it holds pictures of:
    of five well-known tracks sampled, three carried no track image, but all
    five named their album. Without the fallback the provider would miss most
    of what it actually knows. This is the album art for the song being played,
    and the same thing the Last.fm song provider returns.
  * Track lookups are cached both ways, as artist and album lookups already
    were, so a track TheAudioDB does not know is asked about once rather than
    on every play.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 14:20:10 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.16.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A stop arriving in the first moments of startup is no longer ignored.
    The flag telling the signal handler to stand aside for the webserver was
    set immediately before launching it, but Rocket does not begin watching
    for signals there: it first builds and checks its router, runs its ignite
    fairings and sentinels, binds the port, and only then registers for
    signals. Through all of that the handler deferred to a server that was not
    listening, so the signal reached nothing and the daemon ran on until it
    was killed at the stop timeout -- the behaviour 0.15.1 removed, narrowed
    to a window rather than removed from it. A restart or package upgrade that
    stops the daemon just after starting it is the ordinary way to hit it.
  * The webserver is now prepared in two steps so the means to stop it exists
    before anything can ask for it, leaving no window in which a signal has
    nowhere to go. A startup that fails leaves nothing published, so a daemon
    whose API never came up can still be stopped, as before.
  * There is one signal handler in the process rather than two. The webserver's
    own is switched off and the handler in the main thread owns SIGINT, SIGTERM
    and SIGHUP throughout: while the server runs the signal is passed to it so
    its grace and mercy periods are honoured, and otherwise the handler ends
    the process itself. This replaces an arrangement that depended on the two
    handlers chaining in a particular order and on each standing aside at the
    right moment.
  * A stop asked for while the webserver is still starting ends the daemon
    rather than waiting for a shutdown that cannot arrive. The port is bound
    late in startup, so a signal sent while an outgoing instance still holds
    it is passed to a server that then fails to start, and nothing would have
    reported a shutdown that never began.
  * A stop asked for before the webserver starts stops it before it serves.
    The signal handler is registered early, and configuration loading and
    controller setup sit between that and the point where the main thread
    begins waiting -- seconds on a Raspberry Pi. A signal arriving in that
    stretch used to leave the main thread on its way out while the API thread
    went on to bind the port and serve, so process exit tore down a running
    webserver with no grace period and no shutdown fairings. The request is
    now seen before the server is launched.
  * The force-exit watchdog is armed by every shutdown signal. It previously
    sat on only one of the two paths, so a shutdown that stalled while the
    webserver owned the signals had no backstop at all. It now allows eight
    seconds -- longer than the six a graceful stop can take, shorter than the
    ten systemd allows before killing -- and the shutdown request is made
    before the watchdog is armed, so a failure to arm it cannot stop the
    daemon being asked to stop.
  * The webserver's shutdown grace and mercy periods are pinned rather than
    left to the defaults. They were readable from Rocket.toml in the working
    directory and from the environment, and the watchdog above is sized
    against them, so a longer grace set from outside would have had it force
    an exit in the middle of a shutdown that was proceeding normally.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 13:49:14 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.16.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Radio streams get the cover art of the track being played, not just the
    station's logo. A stream carries no artwork of its own, so the MPD backend
    fills cover_art_url with the station logo; that logo then blocked every
    better source. The Last.fm plugin fetches track info on each song change
    and already pulled the album image out of it, but assigned it only when
    cover_art_url was empty, which for a stream it never is. On a device
    playing ByteFM, Last.fm returned a 1200x1200 cover for the track on air
    while the daemon showed a 96x96 favicon. The better image is published on
    the WebSocket as a song_information_update; /api/now-playing is built from
    the player's stored song, which nothing writes back into, so it still
    reports the station logo.
  * Cover art that describes a URL rather than the track playing from it is
    marked in song metadata as cover_art_source "station_logo", and only such
    cover art may be replaced. The per-URL metadata cache is filled by the
    add_track API, which serves both the queueing of a radio station and the
    queueing of a single track with its own artwork, so the two are told apart
    by name: metadata naming something other than the track now playing -- a
    station name against a song title -- describes the URL. Names are compared
    without case or surrounding space, and an artist-less "Artist - Title" that
    was split earlier still counts as the same name. Where there is no name at
    all the URL decides: a stream's artwork is the station's, anything else
    belongs to the one track it was queued with. Artwork a client supplied for
    the track it queued is untouched, as is anything the player itself
    provided. Whatever replaces a placeholder records its own
    provenance, so a real cover is not taken for one by a later lookup.
    Clients that apply song_information_update as a partial update need no
    change; the event contract is in doc/websocket.md.
  * Last.fm answers the Song cover art method. Spotify was its only provider
    and returns nothing until an account is linked, so /api/coverart/song gave
    an empty result for every track on a device without Spotify. The lookup is
    unsigned and needs only an API key: the signed track.getInfo the scrobbler
    uses refuses without a linked account, which would have reproduced the gap
    it was added to close.
  * Cover art for the song being played no longer needs a linked Last.fm
    account either. The lookup that replaces a station logo used the signed
    request, so on a device with only an API key the logo was marked as
    replaceable and nothing ever replaced it. It now falls back to the unsigned
    request, which answers with the album and its images but not with whether
    the track is loved or how often it has been played -- fields that are
    absent from an unsigned answer and are no longer read as "not loved" and
    "never played".
  * The image chosen from a Last.fm album is the largest slot it filled, on
    both paths. Last.fm pads slots it has nothing for with an empty string, and
    the path that replaces a station logo took the extralarge slot alone, so an
    album whose extralarge slot was one of the empty ones left the station
    showing its logo. Both paths now share one selection.
  * The documented example for /api/coverart/methods listed providers that have
    never existed and now shows what the endpoint returns.
  * The 0.15.1 notes explained their own SIGTERM fix wrongly and are corrected
    here rather than rewritten. Their first entry said Rocket's signal handlers
    replace the earlier ctrlc handler, so it never ran; a later entry in the
    same release said, correctly, that Rocket registers through signal-hook,
    which chains onto the earlier handler rather than replacing it. The second
    account is the right one, and the reason SIGTERM went unhandled in 0.15.0
    is the one it gives: the ctrlc dependency's "termination" feature was off,
    so only SIGINT was watched. The same entry's "nothing in the process ever
    got the chance to flush its state" also implied the fix restores flushing.
    It does not: the process now exits cleanly instead of being killed, but no
    player is stopped and no cache is written out on the way.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 13:17:01 +0000

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * audiocontrol exits when it is asked to stop. It never had: every stop,
    restart and package upgrade ended in SIGKILL after systemd's stop timeout,
    so nothing in the process ever got the chance to flush its state. The
    signal was not being ignored -- Rocket received it and shut the HTTP
    server down on time -- but the flag the main thread waits on was only
    cleared from a ctrlc handler that Rocket's own signal handlers replace as
    soon as the server launches. That handler had therefore never run, taking
    with it the force-exit watchdog written to catch exactly this, which sat
    inside it and could not fire. The main loop is now ended by the API server
    returning, which happens precisely when Rocket has finished shutting down.
    A stop takes about five seconds, which is Rocket's own grace and mercy
    periods, rather than the full ten-second timeout followed by a kill.
  * Starting the API server reports which of two things happened rather than a
    bare success. "Rocket ran and has shut down" and "the webserver is
    disabled, nothing was started" were the same value, so the caller had to
    re-read the configuration to tell them apart. Both are now distinct
    outcomes the compiler can check.
  * The startup log no longer claims "API server started on port 1080" from a
    thread that has not started anything. The webserver logs its own address
    when it binds one, which is the code that knows.
  * A shutdown that overruns Rocket's grace and mercy windows also ends the
    process. Rocket reports that case as an error rather than a success --
    outstanding background I/O, which long-lived WebSocket connections
    routinely produce, is one of its causes -- and treating it as a failure to
    start left the daemon spinning until it was killed, which is the behaviour
    the rest of this entry removes, on the shutdown paths a busy device is
    most likely to take.
  * SIGTERM is handled before the webserver starts, and when it is disabled
    entirely. The ctrlc dependency only registers SIGTERM with its
    "termination" feature, which was not enabled, so until Rocket launched
    there was nothing listening for the signal systemd actually sends. Once
    Rocket is up that handler stands aside: Rocket registers through
    signal-hook, which chains onto the earlier handler rather than replacing
    it, so both run on every signal and the earlier one would otherwise end the
    process during Rocket's grace period -- cutting in-flight requests and
    WebSockets after 150ms instead of shutting down over five seconds. The
    handover is symmetric: a launch that fails -- a port already held by a
    previous instance is the ordinary way -- hands the signals straight back,
    so a daemon whose API never came up can still be stopped.
  * SIGHUP shuts the daemon down gracefully rather than being ignored. It is
    one of the three signals the handler covers, so once the webserver is up
    Rocket is told to watch it as well; otherwise it would reach a handler
    that had stood down and no watcher, and a closed terminal would leave the
    daemon running.

 -- HiFiBerry <support@hifiberry.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 14:33:56 +0200

hifiberry-audiocontrol (0.15.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Image and lyrics paths in every payload now carry the externally visible
    API prefix when audiocontrol is reached through a reverse proxy. Album
    cover art, artist thumbnails and the song paths on the WebSocket
    previously carried the internal path while the now-playing response
    carried the external one, so a client needed one rule per endpoint and
    per transport. A path that fell through the proxy answered 200 with the
    web interface's index page rather than an error.
  * List responses declare Vary: X-Forwarded-Prefix.
  * The library version token varies with the client's API prefix, both in the
    ETags on the album and artist lists and in the library_version field of
    /library/<player>. Without that, a prefixed and an un-prefixed response
    shared one validator for one URL, so a conditional request could be
    answered 304 for a representation the client never held, leaving it with
    paths wrong for the route it was on. The field remains opaque and is still
    compared for equality only.
  * A forwarded prefix that is not a plain path is refused, and paths then go
    out in their internal form exactly as they do when no prefix is reported.
    A value beginning with two slashes made every emitted path
    protocol-relative, so a browser resolved cover art against that host
    instead of the device, and a value carrying a query or fragment marker
    swallowed the rest of each path. Control characters are refused with them:
    a URL parser removes tab, carriage return and line feed before parsing, so
    a value containing one could otherwise be reassembled into either shape
    after passing the check.

